WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.220
Since his 1978 debut, Michael Myers has remained&nbsp;
one of the original slasher movie mascots. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:14.100
all, he's already survived multiple decades'&nbsp;
worth of evolution, retcons, and reinventions.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:21.420
It's hard to believe that an evil force of nature&nbsp;
like Michael Myers could actually exist. Based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:26.220
the way Dr. Loomis talks about him throughout the&nbsp;
series, Myers seems more like an ancient figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:31.380
from folklore than an actual human being.&nbsp;
However, director John Carpenter credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:37.080
the creation of Michael Myers to a school trip&nbsp;
where he and his peers visited a mental asylum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:43.260
Carpenter discussed this fateful trip in the&nbsp;
documentary "A Cut Above the Rest," saying,

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:48.000
"We visited the most serious, mentally&nbsp;
ill patients. And there was this kid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.140
he must have been 12 or 13 and&nbsp;
he literally had this look."

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.420
That image stuck with Carpenter, who incorporated&nbsp;
the idea into the original "Halloween" film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:04.020
in which a young Michael Myers stares into the&nbsp;
camera unmasked having committed a heinous crime.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:10.080
It's fairly common knowledge that Michael&nbsp;
Myers' iconic mask was actually an augmented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.640
William Shatner mask. In fact, the script&nbsp;
contained no description of what Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.140
Myers' mask should actually look like.&nbsp;
While on a panel at the Fright Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.540
Film Fest and Horror Weekend August&nbsp;
2008, filmmaker Tommy Lee Wallace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.140
who worked behind-the-scenes on the&nbsp;
original "Halloween," explained,

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:33.720
"The script asked for a blank-faced&nbsp;
mask [...] that makes you feel eerie."

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.940
The killer was also never referred to as&nbsp;
Michael Myers, and instead was simply called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:44.220
"The Shape." With no specific instructions,&nbsp;
Wallace went to a costume shop on Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.600
Boulevard looking for the perfect mask. He&nbsp;
selected the fated William Shatner mask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.960
choosing the Emmett Kelly clown&nbsp;
character Weary Willie as a backup.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.940
While they ultimately picked the Shatner&nbsp;
mask, young Michael does wear a clown mask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:01.320
when murdering his sister. Wallace&nbsp;
would later bring an eerie clown to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.760
the screen when he directed the 1990&nbsp;
TV adaptation of Stephen King's "It."

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:11.400
Since Michael Myers is just a guy in a&nbsp;
mask, almost anyone could play the part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.500
John Carpenter's longtime friend Nick Castle&nbsp;
is credited as "The Shape" in the first film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.060
but he wasn't the only actor to&nbsp;
play the role in 1978's "Halloween."

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:26.580
During the movie's opening, a young actor&nbsp;
named Will Sandin plays a 6-year-old Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:32.460
who brutally murders his older sister. We see&nbsp;
his face when the clown mask is ripped away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:37.380
for a shocking reveal. During the climax of&nbsp;
the film, Michael's mask is torn off again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:42.000
and this time, he's played by actor Tony&nbsp;
Moran. However, they weren't the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.460
actors responsible for bringing one of cinema's&nbsp;
greatest boogeymen to life in the original movie.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:52.320
In fact, producer and co-writer Debra Hill&nbsp;
had to step in to play the role of 6-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.880
Michael's hands. The point-of-view sequence&nbsp;
was so complicated that the filmmakers were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:03.000
unable to bring in another child to be Michael's&nbsp;
hands. In Adam Rockoff's book "Going to Pieces:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.220
The Rise and Fall of the Slasher&nbsp;
Film, 1978-1986," Hill explained,

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.460
"We couldn't afford another kid to&nbsp;
hold the knife later that night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:18.600
so that's my hand holding the knife that stabs&nbsp;
Michael Myers' sister. I had the littlest hands."

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:24.060
The antagonistic relationship between Dr.&nbsp;
Loomis and Michael Myers provides the mythic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.920
undertones of the "Halloween" series. Loomis&nbsp;
is determined to end Michael's reign of evil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.280
especially as it was Loomis who&nbsp;
tried to treat Michael following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.480
the original murder. Basically, no&nbsp;
one knows Michael better than Loomis.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.340
"If you don't find him in&nbsp;
four hours, I'm sure I will."

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.320
That connection runs even deeper when you&nbsp;
realize that the actor who played Loomis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.880
Donald Pleasence, worked with stuntman&nbsp;
Dick Warlock, who portrayed Michael in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.260
the original "Halloween II," on the&nbsp;
1975 Disney film "Escape to Witch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.240
Mountain." Discussing his time working with&nbsp;
Pleasence, Warlock told Halloween Daily News,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:05.100
"I found him to be a gentleman. People&nbsp;
have said that he had a sense of humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:12.660
but I never saw it. When he came on set, he was&nbsp;
ready to work, he knew your dialogue and mine. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:18.000
was the consummate professional. You couldn't&nbsp;
find anybody who was better to work with."

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.240
"Halloween III: Season of the Witch" is usually&nbsp;
referred to as "The 'Halloween' movie without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.920
Michael Myers." On the surface, this is a&nbsp;
totally fair statement. While "Halloween"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:33.660
and "Halloween II" are one continuous story&nbsp;
detailing the night Michael Myers came home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:38.640
the third installment was intended to take the&nbsp;
franchise in a totally different direction. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.240
the documentary "Stand Alone: The Making&nbsp;
of 'Halloween III: Season of the Witch,'"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.980
director Tommy Lee Wallace explained the&nbsp;
decision to create an anthology movie, saying,

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:52.380
"Each year, let's come out with a new movie&nbsp;
on the subject of Halloween, All Hallows' Eve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.400
and everything that comes with it. It&nbsp;
could go in any direction it wants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.360
and each one could spinoff any&nbsp;
number of their own sequels."

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.580
"And I don't know what the hell is going on."

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:08.160
At the time, the film wasn't a hit with&nbsp;
moviegoers, and everyone's favorite masked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.660
killer was brought back in "Halloween&nbsp;
4: The Return of Michael Myers." Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:17.700
it could be said that Myers' presence is&nbsp;
felt throughout the movie, as Dick Warlock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:22.980
who played Michael in "Halloween II," appears&nbsp;
in a new role. Plus, a TV spot for the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:28.500
1978 film featuring Michael Myers is shown&nbsp;
on television in "Halloween III." He may not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:33.300
be the huge presence audiences were hoping&nbsp;
for, but he still made it into the film.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:40.080
One of the few actors to play Michael Myers more&nbsp;
than once is George P. Wilbur, who portrayed The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.700
Shape in "Halloween 4: The Return of Michael&nbsp;
Myers" and "Halloween: The Curse of Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:49.860
Myers." Interestingly, the stuntman credits&nbsp;
John Wayne with his career in show business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:55.320
After serving in the navy, Wilbur got a job at&nbsp;
a dude ranch in Tucson, telling Racks &amp; Razors,

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.960
"They were filming the movie 'El Dorado' nearby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:02.820
so I went to the set and got a job&nbsp;
as an extra and a stand-in for John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:07.440
Wayne [...] Shortly afterward, I moved to Los&nbsp;
Angeles to pursue a career as a stuntman."

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.180
That career certainly paid off&nbsp;
as he went on to add over 100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:17.580
credits to his name and got to play&nbsp;
The Shape on two separate occasions.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:24.660
"Halloween 5: The Revenge of Michael Myers"&nbsp;
is a flawed film, to say the least. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:28.920
the fourth installment did an excellent job&nbsp;
of bringing the franchise back to basics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:33.720
part five took the series into surreal&nbsp;
territory. One of the movie's more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:38.340
bizarre concepts depicts a mysterious Man&nbsp;
in Black with the same tattoo as Michael,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:44.460
roaming around Haddonfield and breaking Michael&nbsp;
out of prison. In the sixth film, the Man in Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:50.545
was revealed to be Dr. Terence Wynn, a character&nbsp;
with only one scene in the original "Halloween."

00:06:50.545 --> 00:06:52.596
"I protect Michael."

00:06:52.596 --> 00:06:56.400
However, that wasn't always the&nbsp;
intention. In fact, Don Shanks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.060
who played Michael in the fifth&nbsp;
film, revealed that the movie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.620
director originally had different plans for the&nbsp;
character. As Shanks told Halloween Daily News,

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.600
"I was going to be the Man in Black because&nbsp;
I played him too. I did bits and pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.680
I didn't do all of him, but there&nbsp;
would be scenes where I'd go out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.000
one door here and I'd just put on the&nbsp;
coat and come through the other door."

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.700
However, this idea doesn't make a lot of sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:25.680
which is probably why it was dropped. As&nbsp;
underwhelming as the Dr. Wynn reveal was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:31.020
having Michael be the Man in Black&nbsp;
would've just been plain confusing.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:37.500
In 1998, "Halloween H20: 20 Years Later" came&nbsp;
out, rebooting the franchise and creating a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:42.540
brand new timeline in the process. The idea to&nbsp;
continue Laurie Strode's story two decades on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.920
actually came from Jamie Lee Curtis herself,&nbsp;
as she told Entertainment Weekly in 2018,

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:53.160
"When Halloween was, like, 19 years old, I&nbsp;
remember calling John [Carpenter] and Debra [Hill]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:59.040
and we had lunch [...] I said to them, 'Guys,&nbsp;
the movie’s going to be 20 years old next year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:06.060
and we’re all still doing the job 20 years later.'&nbsp;
I said to them, 'Why don’t we revisit it?'"

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:11.700
"Halloween H2O" ignored everything that took&nbsp;
place after "Halloween II," and it was supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:17.160
to depict Laurie taking down Michael Myers&nbsp;
once and for all. As the project continued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:22.740
however, Curtis started reading scripts that&nbsp;
left Michael's demise more ambiguous. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:26.400
when she learned that producer Moustapha Al&nbsp;
Akkad, who had control over the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.780
included a clause in the contract that&nbsp;
said Michael Myers cannot be killed.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:36.240
A compromise was made, and a new ending&nbsp;
was produced in which Laurie appears to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.340
chop Michael's head off. The idea was it would&nbsp;
make the audience feel like Michael is finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:46.800
officially dead, but it could also easily&nbsp;
be explained away in a sequel. Indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:50.940
its successor, "Halloween: Resurrection"&nbsp;
revealed that Laurie had actually killed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:56.220
man that Michael had dressed in his clothes,&nbsp;
allowing the murderer to escape. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.220 --> 00:09:01.260
the audience of "Halloween H20" was&nbsp;
robbed of a truly satisfying ending.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:03.858
"Oh my God, she killed the wrong person."

00:09:03.858 --> 00:09:08.100
When the time came to officially reboot&nbsp;
the "Halloween" franchise in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:13.980
Rob Zombie was brought in to remake the original&nbsp;
film in his own vision. He decided his Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:20.400
Myers should be the most visually intimidating&nbsp;
Michael to date, so he cast the 6-foot 9-inch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:26.160
actor and former professional wrestler Tyler&nbsp;
Mane. Although his face is obscured throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:31.860
most of the film, some viewers recognized&nbsp;
him as Sabertooth from 2000's "X-Men."

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:38.220
Zombie's 2007 remake of "Halloween" and&nbsp;
its 2009 follow-up are much darker and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:44.760
more brutal than the original. Aside from a&nbsp;
handful of scary moments, a young person could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:50.220
watch John Carpenter's "Halloween" and walk&nbsp;
away relatively unscathed. Zombie's reboot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:57.060
however, is incredibly violent and crude,&nbsp;
so much so that even the child actor playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:01.920
a young Michael Myers in the movie wasn't&nbsp;
allowed to read the entire script. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:05.280
an interview with Movieweb to promote&nbsp;
the film, actor Daeg Faerch explained,

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:11.400
"I only read my part. And then my mom read&nbsp;
it and said, 'You're not gonna read that."

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:16.680
And once the film was finished, Faerch still&nbsp;
wasn't allowed to see it. The young actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:21.360
revealed that he would be allowed to watch the&nbsp;
beginning of the film, but would have to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:27.420
the premiere and go do something else after that,&nbsp;
on account of Zombie's flick being too gruesome.

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:28.860
"I am going to be kept away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:32.580
even for the premiere. I'm gonna watch&nbsp;
probably the first 10 or 15 minutes."

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:39.420
Given the film's onslaught of profanity, grotesque&nbsp;
violence, and grisly murders, it's easy to see why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:43.680
Faerch wasn't allowed to watch the entire&nbsp;
movie, despite playing such a major role.

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:47.760
2018's "Halloween," which was&nbsp;
directed by David Gordon Green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:53.040
gloriously rebooted the franchise by&nbsp;
largely ignoring all of its sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:58.620
even "Halloween II." The filmmakers worked hard&nbsp;
to honor the original 1978 film by bringing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:02.700
back Jamie Lee Curtis, and enlisting&nbsp;
John Carpenter to score the new movie.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:08.700
In another important nod for hardcore fans, Nick&nbsp;
Castle was also brought back to occasionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:13.200
embody the role of Michael Myers, which he&nbsp;
helped originate. Although Castle didn't play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:18.600
the character for any large stretch of time in&nbsp;
2018's "Halloween," fans loved his inclusion in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:23.640
the movie. Although Castle wasn't on set for&nbsp;
the filming of "Halloween Kills," his voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:29.880
or should we say breath, was used&nbsp;
extensively. As James Jude Courtney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:35.040
the actor and stuntman who portrays Michael Myers&nbsp;
in Green's trilogy, told Halloween Daily News,

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:41.280
"So when I'm stalking and you hear the breathing&nbsp;
in the mask, it's Nick Castle breathing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:46.140
which I think again is very poetic,&nbsp;
because Nick is the spirit of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:52.440
character [...] It's Nick's spirit that I reached&nbsp;
in and grabbed to manifest the 2018 character."

